WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.290
After months of speculation, Alien: Covenant
has finally been unleashed upon the world

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:05.890
in all its gory glory.

00:00:05.890 --> 00:00:09.420
Whatever your feelings are about the return
to the franchise, there are still some considerable

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:11.970
inconsistencies in the story to discuss.

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:15.760
That's why we've rounded some plot holes you
could fit a chestburster through.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:24.280
"Hello my baby, hello my honey, hello my
ragtime gal!"

00:00:24.289 --> 00:00:29.460
But before you keep watching, just be aware:
there are major spoilers ahead, so please...tread

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:30.260
carefully.

00:00:32.620 --> 00:00:33.810
Not like that guy.

00:00:33.810 --> 00:00:35.180
That guy's dead.

00:00:35.180 --> 00:00:36.180
Not so handy

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:40.190
Alien: Covenant's big plot twist at the end
comes with the reveal that it's actually the

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:45.460
nefarious David aboard the ill-fated Covenant,
not his kinder synthetic "brother" Walter.

00:00:45.460 --> 00:00:49.500
In order for David to effectively fool the
surviving crew, he must assume Walter's identity

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.750
completely, and that includes cutting off
his hair… and one of his hands.

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:57.520
Walter notably lost his own hand while protecting
Daniels earlier in the film, but something

00:00:57.520 --> 00:00:59.420
about that doesn't completely add up.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:03.020
When Walter and David duke it out on the surface
of the planet, we learn that Walter's been

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:06.840
built with self-healing technology, allowing
him to recover from being fatally stabbed

00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:07.840
in the neck.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:11.150
So if he can recover from mortal injuries
in minutes...why can't he heal a dismembered

00:01:11.150 --> 00:01:12.700
limb over the course of the film?

00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:17.439
Sure, regrowing a hand is probably tough...but
so is coming back from the dead.

00:01:17.439 --> 00:01:19.359
Safety first

00:01:19.359 --> 00:01:23.259
Sci-fi and action films have caused us to
become accustomed to watching smart characters

00:01:23.259 --> 00:01:25.829
do incredibly dumb things for our entertainment.

00:01:25.829 --> 00:01:27.909
And Alien: Covenant is no exception.

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:31.590
From Oram sticking his head somewhere no reasonable
person would ever even want to approach, to

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:35.659
the crew following David into the cave of
nightmares he calls a home, the movie is loaded

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:37.799
with maddeningly stupid moments.

00:01:37.799 --> 00:01:41.110
But the decision that stands out among all
the others is the one that sees the entire

00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:44.899
crew not bothering to don helmets once they've
landed on the Engineers' planet.

00:01:44.899 --> 00:01:48.920
This is a foreign, unstudied ecosystem, and
there's no guarantee the humans will be able

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:51.259
to, or even should, breathe the air there.

00:01:51.259 --> 00:01:54.729
The lack of protective gear leaves a pretty
convenient entry point for the death spores

00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:57.450
into unsuspecting characters' ears and noses.

00:01:57.450 --> 00:02:01.119
But logically speaking, nobody would set foot
on an uncharted planet without first making

00:02:01.119 --> 00:02:05.219
sure they were completely protected from the
elements, even if the biggest threat is basically

00:02:05.219 --> 00:02:07.299
the world's worst pollen allergy.

00:02:07.299 --> 00:02:08.610
What came first?

00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.080
It's very clearly established in Aliens that
the Queen lays the eggs.

00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:14.340
Both very simple, and very gross.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:17.660
But the over-complicated explanations for
the creatures' beginnings, first introduced

00:02:17.660 --> 00:02:20.360
in Prometheus, are further built upon here.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.030
Alien: Covenant reveals it was actually David
who created them in the first place, Doctor

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.340
Frankenstein-style.

00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:28.700
Ten years in isolation makes a man do weird
things with eggs.

00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:30.390
But how did he engineer them without the Queen?

00:02:30.390 --> 00:02:32.250
Did he create the Queen too?

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:35.260
Is he the creator of all alien lifeforms...or
just some?

00:02:35.260 --> 00:02:39.291
David spends years cross-breeding the neomorphs
in pursuit of a more perfect organism, and

00:02:39.291 --> 00:02:43.400
by the end of Alien: Covenant, we see the
familiar xenomorph — the end result of all

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.750
that experimentation.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:48.750
But if the events of Alien take place about
20 years later, how did the Space Jockey ship

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.720
in that movie end up being filled with eggs?

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:54.010
The more we learn about this mythology, the
less sense it makes.

00:02:54.010 --> 00:02:55.750
Pod people problems

00:02:55.750 --> 00:02:59.860
The catalyst for Alien: Covenant's ongoing
nightmare is an energy surge, which forces

00:02:59.860 --> 00:03:01.940
the ship's crew out of hypersleep early.

00:03:01.940 --> 00:03:05.530
Unfortunately, not everybody makes it out
of their sleep pods in time and Captain Jacob

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:07.960
Branson catches on fire and dies.

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:10.320
"I'm just burnin' up."

00:03:12.020 --> 00:03:13.690
But what actually happened to his pod?

00:03:13.690 --> 00:03:14.930
Did it just malfunction?

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:16.810
Did it have something to do with the energy
surge?

00:03:16.810 --> 00:03:19.530
Can they maybe invent a pod that doesn't double
as a barbecue?

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:22.690
Or did Covenant just want to give a little
gift to the audience in the form of James

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.010
Franco's smoldering corpse?

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.730
"I have a very good life."

00:03:25.730 --> 00:03:27.670
Either way, thank you, movie.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:28.760
Thanks for watching!

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.780
